digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56359-y
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Investigate and evaluate whether epitaxial STO nanolayers can selectively eliminate Ti defect sites on positively polarized PTO facets, create efficient electron-transfer pathways, overcome surface charge-transfer bottlenecks, and thereby enhance overall water-splitting performance in ferroelectric photocatalysts.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) Background ferroelectric charge-separation advantages, evidence of surface/interfacial bottlenecks, and activity gains from surface/interface or defect modification jointly motivate targeted control of surface Ti defects to improve ferroelectric photocatalysis."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) Ferroelectric PTO supports polarization-directed charge separation, and increasing PTO thickness along the polarization direction can enhance the charge-separation driving force."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) Partial elimination of internal screening charges can contribute to improved ferroelectric photocatalytic activity, although the improvement described is moderate."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) Ferroelectric materials are plausibly suitable for photocatalytic study because polarization-light coupling is a research direction and polarization can mitigate electron-hole recombination."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Poor water-splitting performance despite very large internal electric fields makes surface/interfacial charge-transfer limitations a plausible explanation for inefficient delivery of charges to reaction sites."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) Ti defective sites on the positive surface of PTO are important surface-defect targets because eliminating them through STO nanolayer growth creates an efficient electron-transfer route and greatly increases water-splitting AQE."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) The evidence supports engineering or eliminating surface Ti defects on positive PTO facets, including by STO nanolayer growth, to construct efficient electron-transfer routes and enhance ferroelectric water-splitting performance."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Poor water-splitting performance despite very large internal electric fields indicates that the main limitation is plausibly surface/interfacial charge transfer or surface reaction kinetics rather than bulk charge separation."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Given the plausible surface/interfacial bottleneck, a key research question is which surface or interfacial factors limit transfer of photogenerated charges to reaction sites."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) The charge-transfer limitation question motivates studying surface/interfacial charge-transfer or reaction-kinetic factors in ferroelectric photocatalysts."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) For PTO, STO nanolayer growth that eliminates Ti defective sites is a plausible strategy for enhancing electron transfer and improving overall water-splitting efficiency."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) Ferroelectrics possess unit-cell-level structural properties and permanent depolarization fields that motivate applications exploiting their polarization-linked unique physical properties."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Because ferroelectric research targets polarization-light coupling and polarization can reduce recombination, ferroelectrics are plausible candidates for photoelectric or photocatalytic applications."];
  "a1" [label="(0,0,0) If a material research direction targets coupling polarization with light and polarization can reduce recombination, then the material is plausibly suitable as a photocatalyst."];
  "n1" [label="(1,0,0) Ferroelectric materials have garnered considerable attention due to their unique structural properties at the unit cell level for various applications."];
  "n13" [label="(19,0,0) What are the key factors limiting the transfer of photogenerated charges to surface reaction sites?"];
  "n16" [label="(14,0,0) Activity is moderately increased when internal screening charges are partially eliminated, indicating such elimination can contribute to improved activity."];
  "n17" [label="(15,0,0) Growing STO nanolayers on the positively polarized surface of PTO particles enhances overall water-splitting activity by 15×."];
  "n2" [label="(2,0,0) The symmetry-breaking nature of the electrostatic boundary condition generates a permanent depolarization field in ferroelectrics, conferring unique physical properties."];
  "n21" [label="(23,0,0) Eliminating abundant Ti defective sites on the positive surface of PTO through STO nanolayer growth elevates AQE@365 nm of overall water splitting by 400× due to constructing a highly efficient electron-transfer route."];
  "n22" [label="(24,0,0) Precise control of surface defects is a potent strategy for improving ferroelectric photocatalyst performance in water splitting."];
  "n23" [label="(0,0,0) If bulk charge-separation driving force is strong yet overall activity is poor, then the limiting step likely lies in surface/interfacial charge transfer or surface reaction kinetics rather than bulk separation."];
  "n25" [label="(0,0,0) If multiple observations show that changing surface/defect/interface conditions strongly changes activity, then surface defects/interfaces are likely key controllable factors for performance."];
  "n26" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given observations that eliminating screening charges increases activity and STO surface layers boost activity, therefore surface/interface modification is an effective lever to improve ferroelectric photocatalysis."];
  "n27" [label="(0,0,0) If Ti defects on positive PTO facets inhibit electron transfer, then removing or neutralizing those Ti defect sites should enhance electron transfer and improve overall water splitting."];
  "n3" [label="(5,0,0) One major ferroelectric research direction explores coupling between ferroelectric polarization and light, including photoelectric or photocatalytic applications."];
  "n5" [label="(18,2,0) Ferroelectric polarization can mitigate electron–hole recombination and improve photochemical reactivity via internal fields and band bending."];
  "n6" [label="(8,0,0) Before screening in ferroelectrics, carriers predominantly migrate by drifting within the depolarization electric field (distinct from drift/diffusion in other semiconductors)."];
  "n7" [label="(9,0,0) Positive holes and negative electrons move in opposite polarization directions; increasing PTO thickness along polarization enhances charge-separation driving force and augments photocatalytic hydrogen evolution."];
  "n9" [label="(11,0,0) Despite very large internal electric fields (~10^5 kV/cm, far above conventional photocatalysts), ferroelectric catalysts show relatively poor water-splitting performance in practice."];
